# Target C — audited joint-payment / diameter-moment chain, normalized §§134–140

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Lifecycle:** audited-working route module; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

This module is the clean replacement for the collision-heavy research stack formerly
carried by PR #368. Historical drafts reused section numbers already occupied by the
post-123 chain. The unique surviving mathematics is normalized here as §§134–140.

The duplicated Type-II theorem `c_B=0` is not reproved. It is centralized in
[`typeii-loaded-contacts-hull-sides.md`](typeii-loaded-contacts-hull-sides.md): for all
sufficiently large Type-II survivors every loaded-loaded unit contact is a force-hull
side.

Two audit repairs are binding below:

1. in the cap-family payment, the intermediate `+6e_BB` term is retained until §137
   cancels it using `e_BB-C_A<=2d_tile`;
2. in the complement-gap theorem, near-full gap capacity is converted to a support span
   approaching the full gap by quantitative stability of strict superadditivity. No
   exact-equality claim at a fixed positive-area cutoff is used.

Canonical theorem-state authority remains unchanged; all results below are proof-only.

---

# §134 — exact joint payment of attachment routes and bounded defects

For harmonic component `I_r`, write

- `A_r` for the loaded attachment set and `a_r=|A_r|`;
- `L_r` for the occurrence-counted outer walk of the induced harmonic graph `H_r`;
- `d_r=diam(P_r)` for `P_r=conv(A_r)`;
- `f_r,Q_r` for its bounded nonquadrilateral face count and degree excess;
- `P_exc,r=4f_r+Q_r`;
- `c_r` for loaded-loaded unit contacts whose endpoints both lie in `A_r`;
- `T_r,Q_r^+` for the canonical Section-123 augmented-cell quantities; and
- `X_r:=a_r-T_r+Q_r^+`.

Canonical Sections 82 and 123 give the exact local identities

\[
X_r=2a_r-6-2s_r,
\tag{134.1}
\]

and

\[
L_r+Q_r=4a_r-6-2s_r-2c_r.
\tag{134.2}
\]

Therefore

\[
L_r+Q_r-2a_r=X_r-2c_r.
\tag{134.3}
\]

Canonical Section 82 also gives

\[
Q_r\le X_r-c_r.
\tag{134.4}
\]

Every bounded nonquadrilateral face contributes at least one to `Q_r`, so
`f_r<=Q_r<=X_r-c_r`. Hence

\[
\begin{aligned}
L_r+P_{exc,r}-2a_r
&=X_r-2c_r+4f_r\\
&\le 5X_r-6c_r.
\end{aligned}
\tag{134.5}
\]

This is finite and occurrence-safe; bridges and articulation repetitions are already
included in `L_r`.

Put

\[
C_A:=\sum_r c_r,
\qquad
k:=d_H+s_Z.
\]

Canonical Section 123 gives

\[
\sum_rX_r=2(e_{BB}-k-d_{tile}).
\tag{134.6}
\]

Summing (134.5), and then restoring the diameter term, yields

\[
\boxed{
\sum_r E_r
\le
10(e_{BB}-k-d_{tile})-6C_A
+2\sum_r(a_r-d_r),
}
\tag{134.7}
\]

where

\[
E_r:=L_r+P_{exc,r}-2d_r.
\]

No global injectivity of the contacts counted in `C_A` is needed for (134.7): each
local inequality is valid before summation and `C_A` has a favorable sign.

In Type II, canonical Section 83 gives harmonic-cell area saturation

\[
\sum_r area(P_r)={\pi\over4}D^2-o(D^2).
\]

Planar isodiametry gives `area(P_r)<=pi d_r^2/4`, hence

\[
\sum_r d_r^2\ge D^2-o(D^2),
\qquad
\sum_r d_r\ge D-o(D).
\tag{134.8}
\]

Using canonical Section 122,

\[
\liminf {1\over D}\sum_rE_r
\ge c_{det},
\qquad
c_{det}:={\pi\over2}(2-\sqrt3),
\tag{134.9}
\]

we obtain the necessary Type-II condition

\[
10(e_{BB}-k-d_{tile})-6C_A+2a
\ge (2+c_{det})D-o(D),
\qquad a:=\sum_ra_r.
\tag{134.10}
\]

The exact attachment-incidence rewrite used below is

\[
a=q+2c_I-2-d_{tile}
=3q-6-2(e_{BB}-k)-2s_C-d_{tile}.
\tag{134.11}
\]

---

# §135 — deatomized aggregate detour payment

Canonical Section 75 gives

\[
c_I=O(D^{2/3})=o(D).
\tag{135.1}
\]

Thus (134.11) gives

\[
a=q-d_{tile}+o(D).
\tag{135.2}
\]

Combining (134.7), (134.8), and (135.2),

\[
\boxed{
\sum_rE_r
\le
10(e_{BB}-k)+2q-2D-12d_{tile}-6C_A+o(D).
}
\tag{135.3}
\]

Therefore every Type-II survivor sequence satisfies

\[
\boxed{
10(e_{BB}-k)+2q-12d_{tile}-6C_A
\ge (2+c_{det})D-o(D).
}
\tag{135.4}
\]

This removes harmonic-component multiplicity from the linear-scale payment but is not
itself contradictory: the raw loaded-terminal population has enough capacity to satisfy
(135.4).

---

# §136 — capped harmonic-diameter distribution credit

Fix `0<mu<1` and define

\[
k_\mu:=\left\lfloor{1\over\mu^2}\right\rfloor,
\qquad
r_\mu:=1-k_\mu\mu^2,
\]

\[
\boxed{
\Psi(\mu):=k_\mu\mu+\sqrt{r_\mu}.
}
\tag{136.1}
\]

If a Type-II sequence satisfies

\[
\max_r d_r\le(\mu+o(1))D,
\tag{136.2}
\]

then

\[
\boxed{
\sum_r d_r\ge(\Psi(\mu)-o(1))D.
}
\tag{136.3}
\]

Indeed, put `x_r=d_r/D`. Area saturation and isodiametry give

\[
\sum_r x_r^2\ge1-o(1),
\qquad 0\le x_r\le\mu+o(1).
\]

For fixed `sum x_r`, the square mass is maximized by concentrating entries to the cap
`mu` as much as possible. Equivalently, the minimum first moment supporting unit square
mass consists of `k_mu` entries equal to `mu` and one remainder `sqrt(r_mu)`. This is
exact at zero error and stable under `o(1)` perturbations, proving (136.3).

For `1/sqrt(2)<mu<1`,

\[
\Psi(\mu)=\mu+\sqrt{1-\mu^2}.
\tag{136.4}
\]

Insert (136.3) into (134.7), then use (134.11) and (134.9). The result is

\[
\boxed{
6(q+e_{BB}-k)-4s_C-12d_{tile}-6C_A
\ge [2\Psi(\mu)+c_{det}]D-o(D).
}
\tag{136.5}
\]

At the current canonical Section-118 carrier endpoint

\[
\mu_*:=0.9762439137425688\ldots,
\]

\[
\Psi(\mu_*)=1.19291837204486\ldots,
\]

so the right-side coefficient in (136.5) is

\[
2\Psi(\mu_*)+c_{det}=2.80673035132819\ldots .
\tag{136.6}
\]

---

# §137 — completion triangles pay uncovered loaded unit sides

For sufficiently large Type-II survivors,

\[
\boxed{e_{BB}-C_A\le2d_{tile}.}
\tag{137.1}
\]

This is the exact bridge that cancels the loaded-contact term in (136.5).

## 137.1 Outerplanar completion

Triangulate every attachment polygon `P_r` using only its attachment vertices. Pairwise
interior disjointness of the convex cells implies that triangulation edges from
different cells cannot cross properly. Since all loaded vertices are in strict convex
position, no loaded vertex lies inside a triangle of one cell triangulation. Thus the
combined cell triangulations are a noncrossing graph on the `q` loaded vertices with
exactly

\[
N_{cell}=\sum_r(a_r-2)
\]

cell triangles.

Canonical Section 123 defines

\[
d_{tile}=q-2-N_{cell}\ge0.
\tag{137.2}
\]

Complete the combined graph to a triangulation of the full loaded polygon. Exactly
`d_tile` resulting triangular faces are not cell triangles; call them completion
triangles.

Every outer side not used by any attachment cell is incident to a completion triangle.
For large Type II, `q>=4`, and a triangle in a convex-polygon triangulation has at most
two outer sides. Therefore

\[
\#\{\text{outer sides not cell-covered}\}\le2d_{tile}.
\tag{137.3}
\]

The centralized Type-II hull-side theorem identifies loaded-loaded unit contacts with
unit outer sides. A unit outer side is counted by `C_A` exactly when it is cell-covered.
Moreover a positive-area convex cell is unique if it uses a fixed outer side: two such
cells would both contain an inward wedge near the relative interior of that side and
would have overlapping interiors. Hence `C_A` has no multiplicity in Type II and
`e_BB-C_A` is exactly the number of uncovered unit outer sides. This proves (137.1).

## 137.2 Cancellation

From (137.1),

\[
-6C_A\le-6e_{BB}+12d_{tile}.
\]

Thus the left side of (136.5) is at most

\[
6(q-k)-4s_C.
\]

Since the original expression is bounded below, so is this upper bound:

\[
\boxed{
6(q-k)-4s_C
\ge[2\Psi(\mu)+c_{det}]D-o(D).
}
\tag{137.4}
\]

Using `q-k=h-s_Z` and dividing by two,

\[
\boxed{
3(h-s_Z)-2s_C
\ge
\left[\Psi(\mu)+{\pi\over4}(2-\sqrt3)\right]D-o(D).
}
\tag{137.5}
\]

At `mu=mu_*`, this coefficient is

\[
1.40336517566409\ldots .
\tag{137.6}
\]

The cancellation keeps the intermediate `+6e_BB` term until (137.1) is invoked. Any
proof that simply drops that positive term is invalid.

