# §138 — sharp-window wrapping area forces a larger residual diameter moment

This section is conditional on the canonical Section-118 endpoint branch

\[
\max_r d_r\le(\mu_*+o(1))D,
\tag{138.1}
\]

where

\[
\mu_*=0.9762439137425688\ldots,
\qquad
s_*=0.0088,
\]

and the selected wrapping carrier has limiting angular parameter

\[
\theta_*=2.704779235215494\ldots .
\]

Let

\[
G(t):={t-\sin t\over8},
\qquad
\phi:=2\pi-2\theta.
\]

In the sharp Section-118 window, the principal complement gaps obey

\[
g_1\ge\theta-{10\over3}\sigma,
\qquad
g_2,g_3\ge\phi.
\tag{138.2}
\]

Canonical Section 107 gives the exact wrapping-support area formula

\[
area(conv S)={\pi\over4}-\sum_jG(g_j).
\]

Since `G` is increasing on `[0,pi]`,

\[
area(conv S)
\le
A^{up}(\theta,\sigma)
:={\pi\over4}
-G\left(\theta-{10\sigma\over3}\right)
-2G(2\pi-2\theta).
\tag{138.3}
\]

At `(theta_*,s_*)`,

\[
A_w^{max}:=A^{up}(\theta_*,s_*)
=0.480407300085523\ldots .
\tag{138.4}
\]

Under (138.1), the selected wrapping carrier has normalized diameter tending `mu_*`.
Removing its area from the Type-II total leaves residual normalized area at least

\[
{\pi\over4}-A_w^{max}-o(1).
\]

Isodiametry therefore gives residual squared-diameter mass

\[
\sum_{r\ne w}{d_r^2\over D^2}
\ge
R_{diam}-o(1),
\qquad
R_{diam}:=1-{4A_w^{max}\over\pi}
=0.388326427951660\ldots .
\tag{138.5}
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{
\sum_r d_r
\ge
\left(\mu_*+\sqrt{R_{diam}}-o(1)\right)D
=(1.59940234299765\ldots-o(1))D.
}
\tag{138.6}
\]

Replacing `Psi(mu)` by this stronger diameter moment in §137 yields

\[
\boxed{
3(h-s_Z)-2s_C
\ge1.80984914661688\ldots D-o(D).
}
\tag{138.7}
\]

This is an endpoint theorem, not a uniform theorem for arbitrary larger diameter caps.

