# §139 — residual cap-family capacity

Under the same endpoint/common-cap hypothesis, canonical Section 103 leaves at most one
macroscopic wrapping cell. All other macroscopic residual cells are cap type. For a
cap-type circle-supported cell of normalized diameter `lambda<1`, the full cap area is

\[
\boxed{
H(\lambda)
={\arcsin\lambda-\lambda\sqrt{1-\lambda^2}\over4}.
}
\tag{139.1}
\]

Direct differentiation gives

\[
H'(\lambda)={\lambda^2\over2\sqrt{1-\lambda^2}}>0,
\]

\[
H''(\lambda)
={\lambda(2-\lambda^2)\over2(1-\lambda^2)^{3/2}}>0.
\tag{139.2}
\]

Thus `H` is increasing and strictly convex.

The residual normalized area is at least

\[
A_{res}:={\pi\over4}-A_w^{max}
=0.304990863311926\ldots .
\tag{139.3}
\]

Canonical Section 88 makes residual minimal support arcs laminar. Keep inclusion-maximal
arcs. Their interiors are disjoint, every nested cell below one maximal arc fits inside
that circular cap, and small-area cells contribute only `o(D^2)` after the canonical
square-root area cutoff is sent to zero.

To minimize total residual diameter subject to carrying area `A_res`, it is therefore
safe to solve the relaxed finite problem

\[
\min\sum_i\lambda_i
\quad\text{subject to}\quad
0\le\lambda_i\le\mu_*,
\qquad
\sum_iH(\lambda_i)\ge A_{res}.
\]

Strict convexity makes cap area for fixed total diameter largest when diameter is
concentrated greedily: fill one entry to `mu_*`, then one remainder, then zeros. Here

\[
H(\mu_*)<A_{res}<2H(\mu_*).
\]

Let `lambda_2` be defined by

\[
H(\lambda_2)=A_{res}-H(\mu_*).
\tag{139.4}
\]

Then

\[
\lambda_2=0.479113079232518\ldots,
\]

and the two support angles use total angular measure below `2pi`, so the relaxed
optimizer is compatible with the disjoint-maximal-arc constraint.

Therefore

\[
\sum_{r\ne w}d_r
\ge(\mu_*+\lambda_2-o(1))D,
\]

and including the wrapping carrier,

\[
\boxed{
\sum_rd_r
\ge(2\mu_*+\lambda_2-o(1))D
=(2.43160090671766\ldots-o(1))D.
}
\tag{139.5}
\]

The §137 payment then gives

\[
\boxed{
3(h-s_Z)-2s_C
\ge2.64204771033689\ldots D-o(D).
}
\tag{139.6}
\]

A second independent consequence comes from the canonical attachment-contact path bound
`t_r>=d_r+1`. Summing and using `c_I=o(D)` gives

\[
e_{BI}\ge(2.43160090671766\ldots-o(1))D.
\]

The loaded contact-degree cap `e_{BI}+2e_{BB}<=3q` and `e_{BB}=q-m` give

\[
q+2m\ge2.43160090671766\ldots D-o(D).
\tag{139.7}
\]

With canonical Section 75's `s_C=m+k+o(D)`, (139.6) becomes

\[
3q-2m-5k\ge2.64204771033689\ldots D-o(D).
\]

Adding (139.7),

\[
\boxed{
4q-5k\ge5.07364861705455\ldots D-o(D),
}
\tag{139.8}
\]

or equivalently

\[
4h-d_H-5s_Z\ge5.07364861705455\ldots D-o(D).
\]

