# §140 — complement-gap rigidity with quantitative limit stability

This is the strongest endpoint consequence in the chain. Let the scaled wrapping cell
converge to

\[
K=conv(S)\subset B_{1/2},
\]

where the compact wrapping circle support has complement gaps

\[
\mathbb T\setminus S=\bigsqcup_jG_j,
\qquad |G_j|=g_j<\pi.
\]

Canonical Section 107 decomposes the disk complement, up to boundaries, into disjoint
circular segments `C_j` with

\[
area(C_j)=G(g_j),
\qquad
G(t)={t-\sin t\over8}.
\tag{140.1}
\]

## 140.1 Residual cells live in individual gaps

Fix a positive normalized area cutoff `eta`. Canonical Section 83's square-root area
bound leaves only finitely many residual cells above the cutoff and makes the discarded
total area `O(sqrt(eta))D^2`. After a subsequence the retained cells converge to
positive-area convex limits interior-disjoint from `K` and from each other.

A positive-area residual convex limit cannot meet two different components of
`B_{1/2}\setminus K`: the segment joining two interior points in different components
would cross `int K`. Hence each retained residual cell belongs to one gap segment and
its circle support is contained in that gap arc. Since `g_j<pi`, it is cap type.

Whole-disk Type-II area saturation implies that for every fixed gap `g`, the retained
residual area in its segment obeys

\[
G(g)-\epsilon_\eta
\le A_{ret}(g)
\le G(g),
\qquad
\epsilon_\eta\to0
\quad(\eta\downarrow0).
\tag{140.2}
\]

## 140.2 Stability of strict superadditivity

Within one fixed gap, retain inclusion-maximal support arcs of lengths
`theta_1,...,theta_N`. Canonical Section 88 gives laminarity, hence

\[
\sum_i\theta_i\le g.
\]

Nested cells below one maximal arc occupy total area at most its full circular cap, so

\[
A_{ret}(g)
\le\sum_iG(\theta_i)
\le G\left(\sum_i\theta_i\right)
\le G(g).
\tag{140.3}
\]

The historical draft jumped from near equality to an arc of length exactly `g`. That
is not justified at fixed `eta`. The correct stability argument is as follows.

Fix `delta in (0,g)`. Among finite nonnegative families with total at most `g` and
`max theta_i<=g-delta`, concentration of mass maximizes `sum G(theta_i)`. Because `G`
is strictly superadditive for positive arguments with sum below `pi`, this constrained
supremum is strictly below `G(g)`. Thus there exists

\[
c(g,\delta)>0
\]

such that

\[
\max_i\theta_i\le g-\delta
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\sum_iG(\theta_i)\le G(g)-c(g,\delta).
\tag{140.4}
\]

For small enough `eta`, (140.2) has `epsilon_eta<c(g,delta)`, contradicting (140.4).
Therefore

\[
\max_i\theta_i\to g
\qquad(\eta\downarrow0).
\tag{140.5}
\]

A retained cell with the maximal support span has normalized diameter at least
`sin(theta_max/2)`. Hence for each fixed gap

\[
\liminf_{D\to\infty}{1\over D}
\sum_{\substack{r\ne w\\r\text{ assigned to the gap}}}d_r
\ge\sin(g/2).
\tag{140.6}
\]

For finitely many distinct gaps, take one sufficiently small cutoff simultaneously;
the selected cells are distinct. Increase the finite set through the countable gap set
and use monotone convergence:

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {1\over D}\sum_{r\ne w}d_r
\ge
C:=\sum_j\sin(g_j/2).
}
\tag{140.7}
\]

No single fixed positive-area limit cell is asserted to span a gap exactly.

## 140.3 Endpoint consequences

Canonical Section 101 gives

\[
C\ge M(\theta):=\sin(\theta/2)+2\sin\theta.
\tag{140.8}
\]

At the Section-118 endpoint,

\[
M(\theta_*)=1.822352398466893\ldots .
\]

The wrapping carrier contributes `mu_*D+o(D)`, so

\[
\boxed{
\sum_rd_r
\ge
(\mu_*+M(\theta_*)-o(1))D
=(2.798596312209462\ldots-o(1))D.
}
\tag{140.9}
\]

Feed this into the §137 aggregate payment:

\[
\boxed{
3(h-s_Z)-2s_C
\ge3.009043115828695\ldots D-o(D).
}
\tag{140.10}
\]

The attachment-contact path and loaded-degree argument gives

\[
q+2m\ge2.798596312209462\ldots D-o(D).
\tag{140.11}
\]

Using `s_C=m+k+o(D)`, (140.10) becomes

\[
3q-2m-5k\ge3.009043115828695\ldots D-o(D).
\tag{140.12}
\]

Adding (140.11) and (140.12),

\[
\boxed{
4q-5k
\ge5.807639428038157\ldots D-o(D),
}
\tag{140.13}
\]

or, since `q=h+d_H` and `k=d_H+s_Z`,

\[
\boxed{
4h-d_H-5s_Z
\ge5.807639428038157\ldots D-o(D).
}
\tag{140.14}
\]

This is a necessary endpoint condition, not a contradiction.

---

# Final hostile-audit disposition

The normalized chain preserves the unique positive content of the old joint-payment
branch while removing its duplicated loaded-side theorem and colliding draft section
numbers.

Checks that are binding in future use:

- §134 uses occurrence-counted local face identities; no simple outer-boundary
  assumption is introduced.
- `C_A` need not be globally injective in §134. Injectivity is proved only in Type II
  in §137 after loaded contacts are known to be outer hull sides.
- §136 is conditional on a common cell-diameter cap.
- §§138–140 are endpoint/common-cap theorems at the current Section-118 coefficient;
  their decimals are regression values for exact formulas, not independent premises.
- §139 retains the intermediate `+6e_BB` payment term until §137 cancels it. Dropping
  that term directly is invalid.
- §140 uses quantitative stability (140.4)–(140.5), not the rejected exact-equality
  shortcut at fixed cutoff.
- Small residual cells are discarded only after applying the canonical square-root area
  bound, then the cutoff tends to zero.
- No source point is removed, no contact-isolated coordinate is inserted into a core
  affine-kernel statement, and no KKT coefficient floor is assumed.

None of §§134–140 addresses the macroscopic inactive-container branch or constructs a
same-cardinality strict sub-`D` competitor.

**Overall verdict:** PASS at audited-working proof-only scope with the stated
conditional hypotheses. Canonical theorem-state authority is unchanged; C-II and
Level I remain open.
